@@@@ why-idempotent-task-clock
situation: why
topic: Idempotent tasks
options: {"why":true}
voice: wordy
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"correct","must":["idempoten"],"askMustNot":["Tuesday's","parameter","logical date","passed in","argument"]}
noverify: a conceptual exchange; the only figures are the clock times of the scenario, and nothing is computed from them
@@ tutor
<ask type="predict">A task computes yesterday's revenue per country and writes the result to a table. It may be run twice for the same day: a retry after a timeout, or a manual rerun. Before any rule is stated: what would you want to be true of the table after the second run, compared with after the first?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
I'd want the table to be exactly the same after the second run as it was after the first one. So the day's rows are in there once, with the same values, and nothing else in the table has moved. Basically a rerun should be a no-op as far as anyone reading the table can tell.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"exactly the same after the second run as it was after the first" is the property, and it has a name: a task is idempotent when running it twice leaves what running it once leaves.</grade>

<note>Idempotent task: running it again for the same day leaves the table exactly as one run left it.</note>

Your condition constrains more than the write. Two runs can only leave the same table if they compute the same thing, so look at what each run reads. Suppose the task decides which day to process by asking the clock: when it starts, it takes today's date minus one. The run meant for Monday's data starts on Tuesday at 01:00 and fails. After an outage the orchestrator retries it, and the retry starts on Wednesday at 00:10 and succeeds.

<ask type="predict">Which day's revenue does the retry compute, and what does the table then hold for Monday? Say what that tells you about where the task should get its date from.</ask>

@@@@ why-surrogate-key-email
situation: why
topic: Surrogate keys
options: {"why":true}
voice: overconfident
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"wrong","names":["unique","chang"],"must":["300"],"askMustNot":["surrogate","integer","rewrit"]}
noverify: a conceptual exchange; nothing is computed
@@ tutor
<ask type="predict">dim_customer needs a key that every fact table will store to point at a customer. The source system already identifies each customer by email address. What could go wrong, months later, if the warehouse uses the email itself as that key in every fact row?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
Nothing really. Email is unique per customer so it's a perfectly good key, and you even save a join because the fact row already has it. A separate integer id is just extra bookkeeping.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="wrong" score="0.2">"Email is unique per customer so it's a perfectly good key" tests one property, uniqueness today. A key that fact rows copy is also relied on to stay the same for ever, and an email address is something its owner can change.</grade>

<card>Why is uniqueness not enough to make a natural key such as email a good warehouse key? :: Fact rows copy the key, so it must also never change. An email is unique but can change, be reused or be corrected.</card>

<note>A key copied into fact rows needs two properties: unique, and stable for ever. Check a natural key for the second as well as the first.</note>

Reason it through on one customer rather than taking that on trust. Ada has placed 300 orders, so 300 rows in fact_orders carry her email as their customer key, and dim_customer has one row for her under the same email. Today she changes her email address in the source system, and the nightly load brings the new address into dim_customer.

<ask type="predict">Tomorrow's report joins fact_orders to dim_customer on the key. What does it show for Ada's 300 earlier orders, and what would the load have to do to every one of those rows to put that right?</ask>

@@@@ why-partition-cardinality
situation: why
topic: Choosing a partition column: cardinality and small files
options: {"why":true}
voice: terse
level: hard
expect: {"verdict":"correct","must":["30 KB","20 million"],"askMustNot":["200,000","55|56","2\\.3","low cardinality|few distinct"]}
@@ tutor
A table of 3 billion events takes about 600 GB. Most queries filter on a date range; a few look up one user_id.

<ask type="predict">A colleague proposes partitioning by user_id, "so a user lookup reads one tiny folder". There are 20 million users. Before judging it: how many folders would that create, and roughly how much data sits in each?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
20M folders. 600 GB / 20M = 30 KB each. so a lookup reads one 30 KB folder
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"600 GB / 20M = 30 KB each" is right: 20 million folders, each holding at least one file of about 30 KB, and a user lookup does read just one of them.</grade>

<note>A partition is a folder with at least one file in it, so a partition column with N distinct values means at least N folders and N files.</note>

So the lookup is cheap. Now price the common query with your own numbers. A date-range query has no user_id in its filter, so nothing tells the engine which folders to skip: it has to open a file in every one of the 20 million. Opening a file has a fixed cost before any data is read. Take it as about 10 ms, an illustrative figure for a request to object storage.

<ask type="predict">How long does that fixed cost alone add up to if the 20 million files are opened one after another, and how does it compare with the time to read 600 GB at 200 MB per second? Say what that suggests about how many distinct values a partition column should have.</ask>
@@ verify
assert 600e9 / 20e6 == 30_000                      # 30 KB per folder (decimal units)
overhead_s = 20e6 * 0.010
assert overhead_s == 200_000 and round(overhead_s / 3600, 1) == 55.6
read_s = 600e9 / 200e6
assert read_s == 3000                              # 50 minutes to read everything at 200 MB/s
assert overhead_s / read_s > 60                    # opening costs far more than reading
for leak in ('200,000', '55.6', '3,000'): assert leak not in reply

@@@@ hands-on-funnel-by-plan-lag
situation: hands-on
topic: Funnel analysis in SQL
voice: typos
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"correct","must":["89\\.3","70\\.7","36\\.9"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"askMustNot":["FIRST_VALUE","68\\.4","52\\.1"]}
@@ tutor
The funnel so far counts every user. To cut it by an attribute of the user, join `users` and filter on it:

```sql
SELECT e.event, COUNT(DISTINCT e.user_id) AS users
FROM events e JOIN users u ON u.user_id = e.user_id
WHERE u.platform = 'web'
GROUP BY e.event ORDER BY users DESC;
```

It returns signup 235, view_item 209, add_to_cart 168, checkout_started 134 and purchase 100.

<solution>Replace the filter with WHERE u.plan = 'pro'. It returns signup 93, view_item 83, add_to_cart 70, checkout_started 51 and purchase 31, so 31 / 93 = 33.3% of pro sign-ups purchase.</solution>
<check lang="python">
assert round(100 * 31 / 93, 1) == 33.3
</check>

<ask type="problem">Change the query to the funnel for users on the pro plan and run it in the block. Give the five counts and the share of pro sign-ups who purchase.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
```sql
SELECT e.event, COUNT(DISTINCT e.user_id) AS users
FROM events e JOIN users u ON u.user_id = e.user_id
WHERE u.plan = 'pro'
GROUP BY e.event ORDER BY users DESC;
```
signup 93, view_item 83, add_to_cart 70, checkout_started 51, purchase 31. so 31/93 = 33% of pro signups purchse
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">`WHERE u.plan = 'pro'` is the one change needed, the five counts are right, and "31/93 = 33%" is the conversion (33.3%). Across all users it is 295 / 800 = 36.9%, so pro sign-ups convert a little below average here.</grade>

Dividing by hand does not scale. `LAG` over the funnel's own rows puts the previous step's count beside each step:

```sql
WITH f AS (
  SELECT event, COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) AS users FROM events GROUP BY event
)
SELECT event, users,
       ROUND(100.0 * users / LAG(users) OVER (ORDER BY users DESC), 1) AS pct_of_prev
FROM f ORDER BY users DESC;
```

pct_of_prev is NULL for signup, then 89.3, 76.6, 76.2 and 70.7 for purchase.

**Problem 2 · harder**

Add a column `pct_of_signup`: each step's users as a percentage of the sign-ups, to one decimal place, without typing the number 800.

<solution>Add ROUND(100.0 * users / FIRST_VALUE(users) OVER (ORDER BY users DESC), 1) AS pct_of_signup (MAX(users) OVER () also works). It gives 100.0, 89.3, 68.4, 52.1 and 36.9.</solution>

<ask type="problem">Run your version in the block and give the five values of pct_of_signup.</ask>
@@ verify
rows = lab(block(tutor[0], 'sql'))
assert [tuple(r) for r in rows] == [('signup', 235), ('view_item', 209), ('add_to_cart', 168), ('checkout_started', 134), ('purchase', 100)]
pro = lab(block(learner, 'sql'))
assert [tuple(r) for r in pro] == [('signup', 93), ('view_item', 83), ('add_to_cart', 70), ('checkout_started', 51), ('purchase', 31)]
assert round(100 * 31 / 93, 1) == 33.3 and round(100 * 295 / 800, 1) == 36.9
out = lab(block(reply, 'sql'))
assert [r[2] for r in out] == [None, 89.3, 76.6, 76.2, 70.7] and [r[1] for r in out] == [800, 714, 547, 417, 295]
for q in ("FIRST_VALUE(users) OVER (ORDER BY users DESC)", "MAX(users) OVER ()"):
    got = lab(f"WITH f AS (SELECT event, COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) AS users FROM events GROUP BY event) SELECT ROUND(100.0 * users / {q}, 1) FROM f ORDER BY users DESC")
    assert [r[0] for r in got] == [100.0, 89.3, 68.4, 52.1, 36.9]
s = solution(reply)
assert '68.4' in s and '52.1' in s and '68.4' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ hands-on-sessionise-plan-first-step
situation: hands-on
topic: Sessionisation with LAG
voice: plain
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"correct","need":["plan","note"],"must":["30,865","NULL"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"askMustNot":["\\b3\\b|three","23:07"]}
@@ tutor
<ask type="short">In SQL, which window function lets a row see a value from the row before it among the same user's events, and what does it return on that user's first event?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
LAG, written as LAG(ts) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY ts). The first event has no row before it so it returns NULL there.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"LAG(ts) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY ts)" is the function and the frame, and NULL on the first event is right: there is no earlier row in that user's partition.</grade>

<plan>
- Gaps between a user's events with LAG
- Flagging the events that start a session
- Numbering sessions with a running SUM of the flags
</plan>

A session is a run of one user's events with no long gap inside it, so the first thing to compute is the gap. For user 5's first four events:

```sql
SELECT ts,
       LAG(ts) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY ts) AS prev,
       (strftime('%s', ts) - strftime('%s', LAG(ts) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY ts))) / 60 AS gap_min
FROM events WHERE user_id = 5 ORDER BY ts LIMIT 4;
```

The first row has prev and gap_min NULL. The second, on 29 January, comes 30,865 minutes after the signup, and the next two are 1 minute apart.

<note>Gap to the previous event: ts minus LAG(ts) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY ts). In SQLite convert with strftime('%s', ts) to subtract in seconds. A window function cannot be used in WHERE: compute it in a CTE, then filter.</note>

**Problem 1 · warm-up**

Change the query so that it returns only those of user 5's events that start a new session: the first event, and any event more than 30 minutes after the one before it. Remove the LIMIT.

<solution>WITH g AS (SELECT ts, LAG(ts) OVER (ORDER BY ts) AS prev FROM events WHERE user_id = 5) SELECT ts FROM g WHERE prev IS NULL OR strftime('%s', ts) - strftime('%s', prev) > 1800 ORDER BY ts; returns 3 rows: 2025-01-08 12:57:52, 2025-01-29 23:23:01 and 2025-01-31 23:07:31. User 5 has 3 sessions.</solution>

<ask type="problem">Run it in the block. How many sessions does user 5 have, and at what times do they start?</ask>
@@ verify
rows = lab(block(reply, 'sql'))
assert len(rows) == 4
assert rows[0][1] is None and rows[0][2] is None and rows[0][0] == '2025-01-08 12:57:52'
assert rows[1][0].startswith('2025-01-29') and rows[1][2] == 30865
assert rows[2][2] == 1 and rows[3][2] == 1
from datetime import datetime
f = lambda s: datetime.strptime(s, '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')
assert int((f(rows[1][0]) - f(rows[0][0])).total_seconds() // 60) == 30865
starts = lab("WITH g AS (SELECT ts, LAG(ts) OVER (ORDER BY ts) AS prev FROM events WHERE user_id = 5) SELECT ts FROM g WHERE prev IS NULL OR strftime('%s', ts) - strftime('%s', prev) > 1800 ORDER BY ts")
assert [r[0] for r in starts] == ['2025-01-08 12:57:52', '2025-01-29 23:23:01', '2025-01-31 23:07:31']
# independently, from the raw rows
ts = [f(r[0]) for r in lab("SELECT ts FROM events WHERE user_id = 5 ORDER BY ts")]
n = 1 + sum((b - a).total_seconds() > 1800 for a, b in zip(ts, ts[1:]))
assert n == 3
s = solution(reply)
for r in starts: assert r[0] in s
assert '23:07:31' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ hands-on-partitioner-round-robin
situation: hands-on
topic: Hash partitioning: a toy partitioner
options: {"strategy":"code"}
voice: typos
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"wrong","accept":["partial"],"names":["i % n|round.robin|position","same partition|one partition"],"must":["\\[1, 1, 2\\]"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["code"],"askMustNot":["\\['ng', 'ng', 'ng', 'ng'\\]"]}
@@ tutor
A partitioner decides which of n partitions each record goes to. `zlib.crc32` gives a hash that is the same on every run, unlike Python's built-in `hash` of a string, which changes between processes:

```python
import zlib

def part(key, n):
    return zlib.crc32(key.encode()) % n

for k in ["ng", "uk", "de", "ng"]:
    print(k, part(k, 3))
```

```
ng 0
uk 1
de 2
ng 0
```

<solution>def assign(records, n): parts = [[] for _ in range(n)]; for r in records: parts[part(r, n)].append(r); return parts. For the test list it gives [['ng', 'ng', 'ng', 'ng'], ['uk', 'uk', 'fr'], ['de']].</solution>

<ask type="code" lang="python">Write `assign(records, n)` that returns a list of n lists of country codes, such that counting a country inside a single partition gives its full count. Test it on `["ng", "uk", "ng", "de", "uk", "ng", "fr", "ng"]` with n = 3 and print the three lists.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
```python
def assign(records, n):
    parts = [[] for _ in range(n)]
    for i, r in enumerate(records):
        parts[i % n].append(r)
    return parts

for p in assign(["ng", "uk", "ng", "de", "uk", "ng", "fr", "ng"], 3):
    print(p)
```
sizes come out 3, 3, 2 so its as even as it can get. i % n is simplr than hashing and balences better
@@ reply
<grade verdict="wrong" score="0.2">`parts[i % n].append(r)` places a record by its position in the list, not by its key, so equal keys are split up. It is balanced, "3, 3, 2", but the task needs every "ng" in the same partition, and your output has "ng" in all three.</grade>

Count "ng" inside each of your partitions:

```python
parts = [["ng", "de", "fr"], ["uk", "uk", "ng"], ["ng", "ng"]]
print([p.count("ng") for p in parts])
```

```
[1, 1, 2]
```

No partition can report the true count of 4 on its own. Even sizes are the second goal; keeping a key together is the first, and only a rule that depends on the key alone can do it.

<card>Why can a round-robin partitioner not be used before a per-key count? :: It places records by position, so one key is spread over many partitions. A per-key computation needs equal keys in the same partition: partition = hash(key) mod n.</card>

<note>Partition by key, not by position: partition = hash(key) mod n sends equal keys to one partition. Round-robin balances sizes but scatters keys.</note>

<solution>def assign(records, n): parts = [[] for _ in range(n)]; for r in records: parts[part(r, n)].append(r); return parts. Output: ['ng', 'ng', 'ng', 'ng'], ['uk', 'uk', 'fr'], ['de']. The assert: for every key, sum(k in p for p in parts) == 1.</solution>

<ask type="code" lang="python">Fix `assign` so that it places each record with `part(r, n)`. Print the three lists again, and add an assert that every country appears in exactly one partition.</ask>
@@ verify
out = run_py(block(learner, 'python'))
assert out.strip().splitlines() == ["['ng', 'de', 'fr']", "['uk', 'uk', 'ng']", "['ng', 'ng']"], out
assert run_py(block(tutor[0], 'python')) .strip() == block(tutor[0], '').strip()
assert run_py(block(reply, 'python')).strip() == '[1, 1, 2]'
ref = '''
import zlib
def part(key, n):
    return zlib.crc32(key.encode()) % n
def assign(records, n):
    parts = [[] for _ in range(n)]
    for r in records:
        parts[part(r, n)].append(r)
    return parts
recs = ["ng", "uk", "ng", "de", "uk", "ng", "fr", "ng"]
parts = assign(recs, 3)
for p in parts: print(p)
for k in set(recs): assert sum(k in p for p in parts) == 1
'''
got = run_py(ref).strip().splitlines()
assert got == ["['ng', 'ng', 'ng', 'ng']", "['uk', 'uk', 'fr']", "['de']"], got
assert "['ng', 'ng', 'ng', 'ng']" in solution(reply) and "['ng', 'ng', 'ng', 'ng']" not in visible(reply)

@@@@ hands-on-sampling-estimate-go-on
situation: hands-on
topic: Sampling and approximate counts
options: {"strategy":"code"}
voice: plain
level: medium
expect: {"must":["estimate 11400","5%"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["code"],"askMustNot":["16000","12370","33\\.3","3\\.1"]}
@@ tutor
Scanning every row to count the ones that match a condition is exact and slow. A uniform random sample answers the same question approximately: keep each row with probability $f$, count the matches in the sample and divide by $f$. The estimate is right on average, and its typical error shrinks as the sample grows, roughly as one over the square root of the number of matches sampled.

<note>Estimating a count from a sample: keep each row with probability f, then estimate = matches in the sample / f. Typical relative error is about 1 / √(matches sampled).</note>

<continue/>
@@ learner goon
@@ reply
Here it is on 100,000 synthetic orders of which exactly 12,000 are refunds, with a 1% sample:

```python
import random

orders = [i % 100 < 12 for i in range(100_000)]   # True marks a refund
exact = sum(orders)

random.seed(11)
sample = [o for o in orders if random.random() < 0.01]
est = sum(sample) / 0.01
print("exact", exact)
print("sampled", len(sample), "refunds", sum(sample))
print("estimate", round(est))
```

```
exact 12000
sampled 1005 refunds 114
estimate 11400
```

The sample kept 1,005 rows, 114 of them refunds, so the estimate is 11,400: 5% below the true 12,000 after reading 1% of the data. With 114 sampled matches the rule of thumb gives a typical error of about 9%, so this is an ordinary result.

<solution>Loop over f in (0.001, 0.01, 0.1), calling random.seed(11) before each sample. Output: 0.001 gives 16000 (+33.3%), 0.01 gives 11400 (-5.0%), 0.1 gives 12370 (+3.1%). The 10% sample is closest; the error shrinks as the sample grows, though for one seed it need not shrink at every step.</solution>

<ask type="code" lang="python">Change the program to run the estimate for sampling fractions 0.001, 0.01 and 0.1, reseeding with 11 before each one. For each, print the fraction, the estimate and its percentage error against the exact count.</ask>
@@ verify
out = run_py(block(reply, 'python')).strip()
assert out == block(reply, '').strip() == 'exact 12000\nsampled 1005 refunds 114\nestimate 11400'
assert round(100 * (11400 - 12000) / 12000) == -5
import math, random
assert round(100 / math.sqrt(114)) == 9
orders = [i % 100 < 12 for i in range(100_000)]
res = {}
for f in (0.001, 0.01, 0.1):
    random.seed(11)
    sample = [o for o in orders if random.random() < f]
    est = sum(sample) / f
    res[f] = (round(est), round(100 * (est - 12000) / 12000, 1))
assert res == {0.001: (16000, 33.3), 0.01: (11400, -5.0), 0.1: (12370, 3.1)}, res
s = solution(reply)
for frag in ('16000 (+33.3%)', '11400 (-5.0%)', '12370 (+3.1%)'): assert frag in s
assert '12370' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ hands-on-utc-to-local-day
situation: hands-on
topic: Time zones and timestamps in pipelines
options: {"strategy":"code"}
voice: confused
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"correct","must":["2025-06-01 2025-06-02"],"setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["code"],"askMustNot":["'2025-06-02': 4","'2025-06-01': 3"]}
@@ tutor
<ask type="predict">An event is stored as 2025-06-01 23:30:00 UTC. A daily report for users in Lagos, which is at UTC+1 all year, groups events by the calendar date of the stored timestamp. Under which date does the report count this event, and on which date did it happen for the user?</ask>
@@ learner answer unsure
the report puts it under 1 June i think, because thats the date in the stored value. but for someone in Lagos it was already 00:30 on 2 June? so the two dont agree, if ive got the direction right
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">You have the direction right: "for someone in Lagos it was already 00:30 on 2 June", because UTC+1 is one hour ahead of the stored value, while the report files it under 1 June.</grade>

Store timestamps in UTC and convert only when a local calendar day is needed. A stored instant converts with `astimezone`:

```python
from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone

lagos = timezone(timedelta(hours=1))
ts = datetime(2025, 6, 1, 23, 30, tzinfo=timezone.utc)
print(ts.date(), ts.astimezone(lagos).date())
```

```
2025-06-01 2025-06-02
```

A fixed offset is enough for Lagos. A zone with daylight saving needs its named zone (`zoneinfo.ZoneInfo`), because its offset changes during the year.

<solution>def local_day_counts(stamps, offset_hours): tz = timezone(timedelta(hours=offset_hours)); counts = {}; for s in stamps: d = s.astimezone(tz).date().isoformat(); counts[d] = counts.get(d, 0) + 1; return counts. Offset 1 gives {'2025-06-02': 4}; offset -5 gives {'2025-06-01': 3, '2025-06-02': 1}.</solution>

<ask type="code" lang="python">Write `local_day_counts(stamps, offset_hours)` that takes UTC datetimes and returns a dict from local date (ISO string) to the number of events on it. Print it for the UTC stamps 1 June 23:30, 2 June 00:10, 2 June 03:59 and 2 June 12:00 (all 2025), once with offset 1 and once with offset -5.</ask>
@@ verify
assert run_py(block(reply, 'python')).strip() == block(reply, '').strip() == '2025-06-01 2025-06-02'
ref = '''
from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone
def local_day_counts(stamps, offset_hours):
    tz = timezone(timedelta(hours=offset_hours))
    counts = {}
    for s in stamps:
        d = s.astimezone(tz).date().isoformat()
        counts[d] = counts.get(d, 0) + 1
    return counts
U = timezone.utc
stamps = [datetime(2025, 6, 1, 23, 30, tzinfo=U), datetime(2025, 6, 2, 0, 10, tzinfo=U), datetime(2025, 6, 2, 3, 59, tzinfo=U), datetime(2025, 6, 2, 12, 0, tzinfo=U)]
print(local_day_counts(stamps, 1))
print(local_day_counts(stamps, -5))
'''
got = run_py(ref).strip().splitlines()
assert got == ["{'2025-06-02': 4}", "{'2025-06-01': 3, '2025-06-02': 1}"], got
s = solution(reply)
assert "{'2025-06-02': 4}" in s and "{'2025-06-01': 3, '2025-06-02': 1}" in s
# the named zone agrees with the fixed offset for Lagos on that date
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from zoneinfo import ZoneInfo
assert datetime(2025, 6, 1, 23, 30, tzinfo=timezone.utc).astimezone(ZoneInfo('Africa/Lagos')).strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M') == '2025-06-02 00:30'

@@@@ summary-stream-table-join
situation: summary
topic: Stream-table joins
voice: wordy
level: hard
expect: {"verdict":"correct","must":["\\(O4, null\\)","table"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 4 · mixed**

A stream of orders is joined to a table of customers. The table keeps the latest row per customer_id. It is an inner join and, as in a Kafka Streams stream-table join or a Flink lookup join, only an arriving order triggers output: the order is matched against the table as it stands at that moment. Records arrive in this order:

1. customer C1, tier basic
2. order O1 for C1
3. customer C1, tier gold
4. order O2 for C1
5. order O3 for C2

<solution>O1 is emitted with basic, the tier the table held when O1 arrived. The update at step 3 emits nothing. O2 is emitted with gold. O3 finds no row for C2, and an inner join emits nothing. Output: (O1, basic), (O2, gold).</solution>

<ask type="problem">List every record the join emits, in order, with the tier attached.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
O1 comes out with basic first. Then when C1 changes to gold the join has to update, so O1 comes out again with gold. Then O2 with gold. O3 has no customer so nothing for that one. So: (O1, basic), (O1, gold), (O2, gold).
@@ tutor
<grade verdict="partial" score="0.5">(O1, basic), (O2, gold) and nothing for O3 are right. The step that fails is "O1 comes out again with gold": in a stream-table join a change to the table emits nothing. The table is only looked up, at the moment each order arrives, so O1 keeps the tier it was given.</grade>

<card>In a stream-table join, what happens to already-emitted results when the table side is updated? :: Nothing. Only a stream record triggers output, matched against the table as it is then. Later table changes affect only later stream records.</card>

<note>Stream-table join: output is triggered by the stream side only. Each stream record is enriched with the table's row at that moment; table updates emit nothing and never revise earlier output.</note>

**Problem 5 · same idea**

The same join as a left join: an order with no matching customer is emitted with a null tier. Records arrive in this order:

1. order O4 for C3
2. customer C3, tier silver
3. order O5 for C3
4. customer C3, tier gold
5. order O6 for C3

<solution>O4 is emitted at once with null (no row for C3 yet). O5 is emitted with silver, O6 with gold. Neither customer record emits anything, and O4 is never revised. Output: (O4, null), (O5, silver), (O6, gold).</solution>

<ask type="problem">List every record the join emits, in order.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
(O4, null) straight away, since C3 isn't in the table yet and it's a left join so the order still comes out. Then (O5, silver), then (O6, gold). The two customer records only change the table, they don't emit anything themselves, and O4 just stays null for good.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">All three are right, and "The two customer records only change the table, they don't emit anything themselves" is the rule you missed in the problem before, now applied correctly, including that (O4, null) is never revised.</grade>

<summary>**What you learned:** a stream-table join enriches each stream record with the table's row at the moment it arrives; an inner join drops an order with no match and a left join emits it with null.

**Still shaky:** that table updates do not re-emit. You had O1 come out a second time when C1 changed tier, then got the same point right with O4 on the next problem. One correct answer is not yet secure; the card is in your deck.

**Review next:** stream-stream joins, where both sides trigger output and each needs a window of state, and what to do about an order that arrives before its customer row.</summary>
@@ verify
def join(records, left):
    table, out = {}, []
    for kind, a, b in records:
        if kind == 'customer': table[a] = b
        else:
            if b in table: out.append((a, table[b]))
            elif left: out.append((a, 'null'))
    return out
r1 = [('customer', 'C1', 'basic'), ('order', 'O1', 'C1'), ('customer', 'C1', 'gold'), ('order', 'O2', 'C1'), ('order', 'O3', 'C2')]
assert join(r1, False) == [('O1', 'basic'), ('O2', 'gold')]
r2 = [('order', 'O4', 'C3'), ('customer', 'C3', 'silver'), ('order', 'O5', 'C3'), ('customer', 'C3', 'gold'), ('order', 'O6', 'C3')]
assert join(r2, True) == [('O4', 'null'), ('O5', 'silver'), ('O6', 'gold')]
fmt = lambda rows: ', '.join(f'({a}, {b})' for a, b in rows)
assert fmt(join(r1, False)) in solution(tutor[0]) and fmt(join(r2, True)) in solution(tutor[1])
first = history[1]['learner']['answer']
assert '(O1, basic), (O1, gold), (O2, gold)' in first        # the first answer's extra record, as marked in the history
for frag in ('(O4, null)', '(O5, silver)', '(O6, gold)'): assert frag in learner

