**Question 1 of 3**

<ask type="choice" options="The batch layer, recomputed from the full history|The speed layer, because it is the most recent|The serving layer, which recomputes on each query|Whichever layer finished its run last">In a lambda architecture, which layer produces the results that are eventually treated as the correct ones?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
The batch layer, recomputed from the full history
@@ tutor
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"The batch layer, recomputed from the full history": the speed layer only covers the recent data the batch layer has not reached yet, and its results are replaced when the next batch run lands.</grade>

**Question 2 of 3**

<ask type="short">A kappa architecture has no batch layer: one stream job reads an event log and maintains the results. A bug is found in that job's logic. How are the past results corrected?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
cant be. events are gone once the job consumed them. fix the job and only new data is right
@@ tutor
<grade verdict="wrong" score="0.1">"events are gone once the job consumed them" is the belief that fails: reading from a log does not remove anything. The log keeps every event for its retention period, so the fixed job is started from the earliest offset, replays the history into a new output table, and readers are switched to it once it has caught up.</grade>

<card>How does a kappa architecture correct past results after a bug fix? :: Replay: start the fixed job from the beginning of the retained log, write to a new output, then switch readers over. Consuming a log does not delete events.</card>

**Question 3 of 3**

<ask type="short">The log keeps 7 days of events. After a bug fix, a kappa pipeline has to rebuild 90 days of results. What has to be different about the setup for that to be possible, and why can the job not manage it alone?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
retention has to cover the 90 days, or keep the raw events in cheap storage and replay from there. a replay can only read what the log still holds, the job cant recreate deleted events
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"a replay can only read what the log still holds" is the constraint, and both of your remedies work: longer retention, or an archive of the raw events to replay from.</grade>

<summary>**Score:** 2 out of 3.

**Strengths:** the role of the batch layer in lambda, and the link between replay and retention: a kappa pipeline can rebuild only as far back as its log, or an archive of it, reaches.

**Misconception to fix:** that consuming an event removes it from the log. You held it with confidence in question 2 and reasoned correctly from the opposite in question 3; the card is in your deck to make it stick.

**Review:** log retention and replay from the earliest offset, switching readers from the old output to the rebuilt one, and what lambda costs by keeping the same logic in two codebases.</summary>
